cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this paper or its verification engines, please cite this work."
title: "How much dimension does an epsilon-approximation to a non-attained quantum correlation cost? A Diophantine answer for K_5"
type: article
authors:
  - given-names: Seth
    family-names: Douglas
    email: apsiape@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-4708-3252"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-10"
license: CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Dykema, Paulsen and Prakash proved that the finite-dimensional quantum
  correlation set C_q(5,2) is not closed via a K_5 graph correlation function,
  by an argument existential in dimension. We answer the quantitative question
  it leaves open. Let D(eps) be the least block dimension of a
  finite-dimensional carrier whose value exceeds the infimum by at most eps, at
  exact marginals and at the distinguished parameter t = 1/sqrt(5). Then
  D(eps) = Theta(eps^(-1/4)), and the exponent is arithmetic in origin: the
  lower bound is the Diophantine inequality ||q sqrt(5)|| > 1/(5q) transported
  through the law of total variance, and the upper bound is attained by feeding
  consecutive continued-fraction convergents of sqrt(5) into a two-block
  carrier, where the deficit is exactly the product of the two one-sided
  approximation errors. The constant is bracketed to a factor 2.176 in the
  denominator convention, between an unconditional floor 0.4472135955 and an
  attained value 0.9732489895. We also show that orbit topology and attainment
  are different invariants on this object, and contrast K_5 with I3322, where
  the analogous cost is only logarithmic, concluding that dimension laws for
  non-closure witnesses are governed by the arithmetic of the parameter rather
  than by a universal rate.
keywords:
  - sums of projections
  - tracial states
  - quantum correlations
  - non-closure
  - continued fractions
  - Diophantine approximation
  - Coxeter functor
  - Tits form
  - star quiver
  - approximation rate
  - dimension law
  - exact arithmetic verification
